after years of wrangling chile's  congress has passed a bill allowing  same-sex couples to marry and adopt  children until now gay couples could  only enter into a civil union once the  bill is signed by the president chile  will become the seventh latin american  country to legalize same-sex marriage  have been anxiously awaiting the  approval of the marriage equality bill  it will also allow same-sex couples to  adopt children  something fundamental for many families  and for children like the sun  maximiliano  today maximiliano only has one legally  recognized mother that's me that means  if i die my family members would have  custody of him in that case he would  have to move to another house his right  to his family identity would be violated  this is the most extreme case but the  fact that the state recognized our  partnership but not us as a family unit  left us exposed  if we as a couple broke up for example i  would not have the legal right to take  care of my son  he would not be able to demand for  example child support for me  velamur of the lgbtq advocacy group  igualis has been a driving force behind  the bill the legislation had been  delayed by conservative parties who see  marriage only as the union of a man and  a woman  if marriage was not the social and legal  institution that grants more benefits  and protections  perhaps the lgbtq population  would not have to fight for this right  but that is the case the fact that  people can now get married and feel  protected before the law is a tremendous  step in this country  polls show that more than 70 of the  population in chile approves of marriage  equality as well as adoption by same-sex  couples  this dignifies us as a country we as a  country can no longer make this  distinction because we are committed to  the idea that everyone needs to have  better access to rights better access to  opportunities and equality before the  law  those are the things that are  particularly important in chile  rosanna have been together for nine  years they signed a civil union  agreement four years ago but that did  not include equal parental rights the  legal recognition that maximiliano has  two mothers  now the three of them will be able to be  a family before the law  obviously we want to get married  a moment they've long been waiting for  well dw's benjamin alvarez grouper joins  me in the studio to discuss this further  this is a landmark a law but the  same-sex marriage bill was brought in in  2017. why has it taken why has this  taken so long to be implemented there's  been a lot of opposition by conservative  politicians and by conservative parties  this was the law was first presented in  2017 by then president mitchell bachelet  is now the u.n human rights chief but  there was a lot of opposition and the  catholic church played a very important  role and still is playing an important  role it is swifting but still chile is a  very conservative country  when it comes to that so earlier this  year when the conservative president  sebastian piner said the time has come  for  this bill and also for equal marriage in  chile he won praise but also criticism  from within his government coalition to  push further for this law and now today  both chambers gave the green light and  he will sign it and then it will be the  law that two  people of the same sex can get married  so is this an indication of significant  change in chile and its society it shows  indeed that chile is more open to  liberal laws and to progress when it  comes not only to lgbtq rights but also  for example when it comes to abortion a  few years ago chile had a total ban on  abortion and now it's allowed in certain  circumstances so it shows that there has  been a shift in the society but not  everyone is a very big fan of this new  law next week and there will be  presidential elections and one of the  two candidates far-right candidate jose  antonio cass  has criticized is not only an opponent  to same-sex marriage he has called lgbtq  ngo's organization part of the gay lobby  and won the la moner the presidential  palace was illuminated with the rainbow  colors he said that the presidency had  basically surrendered to a gay  dictatorship whatever that means he will  face  a left wing a presidential candidate  he's called gabriel boris who has  praised this vote today and said that  under his government  the lgbtq community will have also full  of rights during the government but it  shows of course that there's also that  the people still opposing  this measure josenton augusta for a  candidate even went further by saying  that the children of same-sex couples  could even sue the state for not giving  them the right to have a mother and a  father so the majority of chileans is  indeed in favor of the new law but there  is indeed opposition and high-ranking  politicians that are still against it  all right ben thank you very much
 

